    "summary": "Let It Grow shines, Eyes → Stella Blue flows, solid jams",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a crisp Let It Grow and an engaging Eyes Of The World → Stella Blue transition. Jerry’s guitar is crystal‑clear while Bob adds driving rhythm throughout. Recording quality is good, making it a solid early‑fall ’73 listening experience.",
    "review": "On September 12, 1973 at William and Mary College Hall the Dead rolled out a relaxed yet rewarding set that captures the band in a steady groove. The first set moves comfortably through classics like Bird Song and Jack Straw, but it’s the second set that truly stands out: a jammy Loose Lucy leads into a tightly executed Weather Report Suite Prelude/Part 1, then Let It Grow erupts with high‑energy guitar work and a Mingus‑like rhythmic push from Bob. The Eyes Of The World → Stella Blue segue is fluid and melodic, showcasing Jerry’s crystal‑clear tone and inventive slide phrasing that follows the extended Eyes jam. Though the show isn’t as wild as some ’73 monster dates, the solid playing, smooth transitions, and strong song selection make it a worthwhile snapshot of the band’s early‑fall form.\nThe best available source is the soundboard capture (gd73-09-12.sbd.sly.14010.sbeok.shnf), which preserves the clarity of Jerry’s tone and the overall balance of the mix while avoiding the splices and muffled sections found on the matrix recording. Fans looking for a clear, representative performance of this era will find this show rewarding, especially for its standout Let It Grow, Weather Report Suite, and the Eyes → Stella Blue sequence.",
